<ExplainerLayout title="Bayesian / MDL Evidence for Probes" breadcrumb="Bayesian / MDL Evidence" section="interpretability-methods">
<Hero title="Bayesian / MDL Evidence for Probes" subtitle="Bayesian evidence and MDL evaluate a probe by balancing label fit against model complexity." />

<p>
Probe accuracy answers one question: how well does this probe predict the labels?
Bayesian evidence and minimum description length ask a different one: how much
prediction did the probe buy per unit of complexity, relative to a baseline? The
complexity term matters for interpretation because a high-capacity readout can fit
structure that belongs to the probe rather than to the representation.
</p>

<p>
A flexible probe can keep raising training or even test accuracy by fitting
accidental regularities. A model-selection criterion charges for that flexibility.
Evidence rises when extra parameters explain real structure and falls when they
mostly memorize, so the criterion and raw accuracy can point in opposite
directions.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 1 · Evidence peaks before raw fit stops improving" canvasId="mdl-evidence-canvas" width="760" height="420" readoutId="mdl-evidence-readout">
